title: list reads line 1, and only claim knows what it means
created: 2026-09-17T16:36:06Z
updated: 2026-09-17T21:34:43Z
effort: M
area: cli
handoff:

survey() decides a ticket is claimed because line 1 is not empty. claim
decides with claim_state, which resolves free, expired, live, dead and
unverifiable. The two disagree, and list is the one that is wrong:

  $ pidly status 1
  1  expired  holder cloud-1  lease until 2020-01-01T00:00:00Z
  $ pidly list          # ready work
  $ pidly list -a
  1     claimed  abandoned lease
  $ pidly claim 1
  1 claimed by ... (previous lease had expired)

So an expired lease is takeable and no listing offers it. The same holds for
a dead claim, which is at least reported by check and cleared by reap; an
expired one needs no clearing, which is exactly why nothing else surfaces it.

The reason list does not ask is cost. survey is one awk pass over the whole
tracker, and claim_state is a shell function: asking it per ticket is a
process per ticket, which is the 17.4s-against-0.44s shape measured in 27.

But the two cases split, and only one of them is expensive:

- expired is a timestamp comparison. The expiry is in the claim line and
  ISO-8601 sorts lexically, so the awk pass can decide it against a now
  passed in with -v. No process, no cost.
- dead needs the process table, so it stays a process per claimed ticket.
  Claimed tickets are few, and it is the case reap and check already cover,
  so list can leave it or resolve it only for the handful.

Doing the cheap half would make list -a honest about the state that is
takeable, which is the one that loses work today.

Related to 42: both are list calling something ready or not ready on
thinner evidence than the command that acts on it.

Done when an expired lease reads as takeable in list and list -a, the awk
pass still makes no per-ticket process to decide it, and the tests cover an
expired lease, a live claim and a dead one.
